1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,475
Block three.

2
00:00:01,475 --> 00:00:04,725
Biology, and why representation matters.

3
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:11,800
On the page, this block is the sections "Biological analogues" and "Representation matters as much as the model".

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,525
Blocks one and two were about models.

5
00:00:14,525 --> 00:00:24,675
This block is about inputs, and its claim is one that I think is the most useful thing in this session: what you feed a model matters at least as much as which model you choose.

6
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:32,050
We will see it four times, in four different decades, on four different problems, and it will be the same lesson every time.

7
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:40,900
We will also meet the biological problem that the rest of the session, the lab and your capstone project are built on: predicting secondary structure.

8
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,150
Let us start by showing that XOR is not a toy.

9
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:51,350
It is a real shape of problem in molecular biology, and here it is in protein-DNA binding.

10
00:00:51,350 --> 00:01:04,975
In two thousand and two, Bulyk and colleagues used protein-binding microarrays to measure how strongly the zinc-finger protein Zif two six eight, and mutants of it, bound to all sixty-four possible central triplets of DNA.

11
00:01:04,975 --> 00:01:07,800
So this is not a model of binding.

12
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,000
It is a measurement of all the combinations.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,225
What they found is that the effect of one position depends on what is at the others.

14
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:20,725
A base that improves binding in one context makes it worse in another.

15
00:01:20,725 --> 00:01:25,700
The slide shows a toy version of that, stripped down so you can see the logic.

16
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:34,725
Imagine a site of two positions, where binding is strong when one position is a purine and the other is a pyrimidine, and weak when they are the same kind.

17
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:37,425
There are sixteen possible sites.

18
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:42,100
Now look at panel b, the sequence logo built from the strong sites.

19
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:46,625
At each position, each base appears in two of the eight strong sites.

20
00:01:46,625 --> 00:01:48,800
Equal frequencies.

21
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,075
The information content is zero bits.

22
00:01:52,075 --> 00:01:56,275
The logo is completely flat, and it tells you nothing at all.

23
00:01:56,275 --> 00:02:00,600
And yet panel c shows the pair determines binding completely.

24
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:05,500
The information is there; it is simply not in either position separately.

25
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,725
That is XOR.

26
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:16,200
Same structure, different costume: the answer depends on whether the two positions agree or differ, and no per-position score can express it.

27
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:26,275
And so a position weight matrix, which is a sum of per-position scores, and a consensus sequence, which is even cruder, both fail here, however carefully you build them.

28
00:02:26,275 --> 00:02:28,900
Not because your counts were bad.

29
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:33,375
Because a sum of independent terms cannot express a dependency.

30
00:02:33,975 --> 00:02:39,875
Now, Block two's answer to XOR was to make the model more powerful: add a hidden layer.

31
00:02:39,875 --> 00:02:44,525
There is a second answer, and it is the one this block is really about.

32
00:02:44,525 --> 00:02:47,875
Leave the model linear, and change the input.

33
00:02:47,875 --> 00:02:55,250
Take XOR, and give the model a third input: x one times x two, the product of the two original inputs.

34
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:58,125
Now a plain weighted sum can do it.

35
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:04,025
The weights one, one and minus two, with a bias of zero, reproduce XOR exactly.

36
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:11,200
Check it: at one, one, the sum is one plus one minus two times one, which is zero, so the output is zero.

37
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,950
At one, zero, the product is zero, so the sum is one, output one.

38
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:17,650
And so on.

39
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:20,250
The model did not change at all.

40
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:23,800
It is still one weighted sum, still picture one.

41
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:28,775
We changed what we fed it, and an impossible problem became a trivial one.

42
00:03:28,775 --> 00:03:32,075
So why is this not the universal solution?

43
00:03:32,075 --> 00:03:39,275
Because of one word in what I just said: someone had to know that the product of the two inputs was the useful thing to add.

44
00:03:39,275 --> 00:03:44,150
For XOR that is easy, because the problem is tiny and we can see it.

45
00:03:44,150 --> 00:03:51,675
For real binding sites, or for secondary structure, nobody knows in advance which combinations of which positions matter.

46
00:03:51,675 --> 00:03:59,900
If you have thirteen residues of sequence, there are an enormous number of possible interaction terms, and almost all of them are useless.

47
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:03,350
That is precisely what a hidden layer buys you.

48
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:10,400
A hidden unit computes some combination of the inputs, and training decides which combinations are worth computing.

49
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:15,325
You are not supplying the features; you are supplying the capacity to find them.

50
00:04:15,325 --> 00:04:17,900
Keep both halves of this slide.

51
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:20,675
Better model, or better input.

52
00:04:20,675 --> 00:04:26,400
The rest of this block is the same choice, played out in the history of a real prediction problem.

53
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,325
So let us meet the problem.

54
00:04:29,325 --> 00:04:31,950
Secondary structure prediction.

55
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:37,125
A protein chain folds into a three-dimensional shape, which was Session six.

56
00:04:37,125 --> 00:04:47,050
Secondary structure is a coarser description than that: it is the local shape of the backbone, in short stretches, without saying how those stretches are arranged in space.

57
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:51,825
There are three classes, and they are defined by patterns of hydrogen bonds.

58
00:04:51,825 --> 00:05:00,475
A helix, usually an alpha helix: the backbone coils, and each residue makes a hydrogen bond to the residue four positions along the chain.

59
00:05:00,475 --> 00:05:06,325
That regular spacing is what makes it a spiral with about three and a half residues per turn.

60
00:05:06,325 --> 00:05:15,325
A strand, part of a beta sheet: the backbone is extended, almost straight, and it hydrogen bonds sideways to another strand lying next to it.

61
00:05:15,325 --> 00:05:23,275
Note the difference: helix bonds go along the chain, strand bonds go between chains or between distant parts of the same chain.

62
00:05:23,275 --> 00:05:26,100
And coil, which is everything else.

63
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:30,025
Turns, loops, and everything that is neither of the other two.

64
00:05:30,025 --> 00:05:33,800
Coil is not disorder; it is simply not regular.

65
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:40,550
The prediction task is then: given the sequence, assign one of those three labels to every single residue.

66
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:43,225
Not one answer per protein.

67
00:05:43,225 --> 00:05:46,900
One answer per position, hundreds per protein.

68
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:52,700
The example on the slide is ubiquitin, chosen because it contains all three classes.

69
00:05:52,700 --> 00:06:03,825
Our usual running example, the beta chain of haemoglobin, is almost entirely helix with no strands at all, which makes it a poor illustration here even though we come back to it later.

70
00:06:04,425 --> 00:06:11,500
The first methods for this are from the nineteen seventies, and here is the one worth knowing, because it is picture one.

71
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:15,700
The GOR method, from nineteen seventy-eight, works like this.

72
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:23,025
To label one residue, look at a window around it: the residue itself and eight on each side, seventeen in total.

73
00:06:23,025 --> 00:06:31,125
Every residue in that window contributes a value to each of three running totals, one for helix, one for strand, one for coil.

74
00:06:31,125 --> 00:06:36,800
How much it contributes depends on which amino acid it is and where in the window it sits.

75
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:41,750
Add it all up, and whichever of the three totals is largest is your prediction.

76
00:06:41,750 --> 00:06:44,450
Now look at that description again.

77
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:50,425
Each position contributes a value depending on which residue it is, and the values are summed.

78
00:06:50,425 --> 00:06:54,500
That is three weighted sums over a seventeen-residue window.

79
00:06:54,500 --> 00:07:01,125
It is three perceptrons without the activation, with the weights derived from statistics rather than learned.

80
00:07:01,125 --> 00:07:07,575
It is picture one, in nineteen seventy-eight, fifteen years before anyone called this machine learning.

81
00:07:07,575 --> 00:07:19,650
For context on accuracy: Chou and Fasman, from nineteen seventy-four, used per-residue propensities plus rules about where helices start and stop, and reached roughly fifty to sixty per cent.

82
00:07:19,650 --> 00:07:25,025
GOR, by summing information over a window, reached about sixty-five per cent.

83
00:07:25,025 --> 00:07:39,050
Both are worth knowing as baselines, and both are reasonable things to compare your capstone project against, because if your neural network cannot beat a method from nineteen seventy-eight, something is wrong with your pipeline rather than with the method.

84
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:43,800
And then, for about fifteen years, that number barely moved.

85
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,275
People tried better rules.

86
00:07:46,275 --> 00:07:48,825
People tried bigger windows.

87
00:07:48,825 --> 00:08:01,250
And people tried neural networks, properly: in nineteen eighty-eight, Qian and Sejnowski trained a network with a hidden layer on windows of single sequence, which is exactly picture two applied to this problem.

88
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:03,875
They reached sixty-four per cent.

89
00:08:03,875 --> 00:08:05,375
Sixty-four.

90
00:08:05,375 --> 00:08:11,850
Slightly worse than GOR's sixty-five, from a method with a hidden layer, trained by backpropagation.

91
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:14,975
A more powerful model bought nothing at all.

92
00:08:14,975 --> 00:08:28,100
And their conclusion is worth quoting in spirit, because it was an honest and correct reading of their own result: they concluded that no method based only on local single-sequence information was likely to do much better.

93
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:30,500
Think about what that means.

94
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,375
They were not saying their network was bad.

95
00:08:33,375 --> 00:08:36,375
They were saying the ceiling was not in the model.

96
00:08:36,375 --> 00:08:38,800
The ceiling was in the input.

97
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:48,775
The information needed to decide the answer was not present in what they were feeding it, and no amount of model capacity can recover information that is not there.

98
00:08:48,775 --> 00:08:53,775
Then in nineteen ninety-three, PHD reached about seventy-one per cent.

99
00:08:53,775 --> 00:08:58,375
A jump of six or seven points after fifteen years of stagnation.

100
00:08:58,375 --> 00:09:00,525
So: what changed?

101
00:09:00,525 --> 00:09:03,275
Hold that question for three slides.

102
00:09:03,875 --> 00:09:08,250
Here is why single sequences hit a ceiling, in one example.

103
00:09:08,250 --> 00:09:16,800
On the slide is a ten-residue stretch: glutamine, glycine, threonine, alanine, valine, valine, valine, serine, alanine, alanine.

104
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:21,575
The same ten letters, in the same order, in two different real proteins.

105
00:09:21,575 --> 00:09:26,950
In a malate oxidoreductase, structure one V L six, that stretch is a helix.

106
00:09:26,950 --> 00:09:33,075
In an antibody heavy chain, structure four J B nine, the same stretch is strand and coil.

107
00:09:33,075 --> 00:09:35,175
Identical input.

108
00:09:35,175 --> 00:09:37,175
Opposite answers.

109
00:09:37,175 --> 00:09:43,025
So ask yourself: how could any method that sees only this window possibly get both right?

110
00:09:43,025 --> 00:09:44,525
It cannot.

111
00:09:44,525 --> 00:09:48,975
Not a better network, not a bigger window, not more training data.

112
00:09:48,975 --> 00:09:55,675
The function it is being asked to learn is not a function at all: the same input maps to two different outputs.

113
00:09:55,675 --> 00:10:03,125
That is what a ceiling looks like from the inside, and it is why the Qian and Sejnowski result was not a failure of effort.

114
00:10:03,125 --> 00:10:11,325
So the real question is the next one: what else do we know about each of these two proteins, beyond this window, that distinguishes them?

115
00:10:11,325 --> 00:10:13,875
And the answer is: its family.

116
00:10:13,875 --> 00:10:16,100
We know its relatives.

117
00:10:16,100 --> 00:10:19,825
Which is the subject of the whole first half of this course.

118
00:10:20,425 --> 00:10:28,200
Before the answer, one practical slide that you will need in every remaining session: how do you turn letters into numbers at all?

119
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:31,725
The wrong way first, because it is the tempting way.

120
00:10:31,725 --> 00:10:37,300
Number the amino acids: alanine is one, cysteine is two, and so on to twenty.

121
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:39,050
Do not do this.

122
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:46,850
It tells the model that cysteine is twice alanine, and that tryptophan is nineteen more than alanine, and that there is an order.

123
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:52,400
None of that is true, and the model will dutifully learn from the false structure you handed it.

124
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:55,325
The right way is one-hot encoding.

125
00:10:55,325 --> 00:11:02,975
Each residue becomes twenty numbers: nineteen zeros, and a single one in the slot for the amino acid that is there.

126
00:11:02,975 --> 00:11:08,400
No order is implied, and every pair of amino acids is equally far apart.

127
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:10,450
The cost is size.

128
00:11:10,450 --> 00:11:18,800
A window of thirteen residues, one-hot encoded, is two hundred and sixty input numbers, for thirteen residues of information.

129
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:22,850
That is how the input counts grow so quickly in these models.

130
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:25,825
And then the one that matters for the next slide.

131
00:11:25,825 --> 00:11:27,250
A profile.

132
00:11:27,250 --> 00:11:38,100
Instead of one sequence, take a multiple alignment of the protein and its homologs, and for each column, record the proportion of each amino acid across the aligned sequences.

133
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:42,900
That is, in effect, the average of the aligned sequences' one-hot vectors.

134
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:53,200
So a profile column is still twenty numbers per position, but now they carry fractions rather than a single one, and those fractions describe a family rather than an individual.

135
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:56,950
Two other options worth naming, since you will meet them.

136
00:11:56,950 --> 00:12:04,575
Physicochemical properties: encode each residue by a handful of numbers such as hydrophobicity, size and charge.

137
00:12:04,575 --> 00:12:10,600
And learned embeddings, where the numbers are themselves learned from data, which is Session eleven.

138
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:17,750
A footnote for the curious: an embedding layer in a library is nothing but a linear layer applied to a one-hot vector.

139
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:23,150
Multiplying a one-hot vector by a matrix picks out one row of that matrix.

140
00:12:23,150 --> 00:12:26,725
So there is less novelty there than the name suggests.

141
00:12:27,325 --> 00:12:30,825
And here is the answer to the question I left hanging.

142
00:12:30,825 --> 00:12:39,400
PHD, by Burkhard Rost and Chris Sander in nineteen ninety-three, took the accuracy from about sixty-five per cent to about seventy-one.

143
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:41,450
What did they change?

144
00:12:41,450 --> 00:12:43,200
Not the model.

145
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:49,675
The model was a network with a hidden layer, which Qian and Sejnowski had already used five years earlier.

146
00:12:49,675 --> 00:12:51,875
They changed the input.

147
00:12:51,875 --> 00:13:00,850
Instead of feeding the network a window of one sequence, they fed it a window of a profile: the multiple alignment of that protein with its homologs.

148
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:11,425
In their own words, the new key aspect is the use of evolutionary information in the form of multiple sequence alignments, used as input in place of single sequences.

149
00:13:11,425 --> 00:13:14,075
Now, why does that help so much?

150
00:13:14,075 --> 00:13:18,925
Because of a fact about evolution that is the backbone of this whole course.

151
00:13:18,925 --> 00:13:22,500
Structure is conserved more strongly than sequence.

152
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:28,400
Structural cores evolve something like three to ten times more slowly than the sequence does.

153
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:37,250
So when you look at a family of homologous proteins, the variation you see is not random: it is the variation that the structure tolerated.

154
00:13:37,250 --> 00:13:41,875
A position in the middle of a helix varies in the ways a helix allows.

155
00:13:41,875 --> 00:13:46,050
A position in a tight turn varies in the ways a turn allows.

156
00:13:46,050 --> 00:13:49,275
One sequence tells you what this protein is.

157
00:13:49,275 --> 00:13:55,550
A family tells you what this position is allowed to be, and that is a far stronger hint about structure.

158
00:13:55,550 --> 00:14:01,850
Go back two slides, to the ten residues that were a helix in one protein and a strand in another.

159
00:14:01,850 --> 00:14:04,000
Their families differ.

160
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,625
The profiles differ, even though the single sequences are identical.

161
00:14:08,625 --> 00:14:15,650
The ambiguity that was unresolvable in the input is resolved, not by a better model, but by a better input.

162
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:23,625
PHD is worth one more slide, because the architecture is a nice example of picture two used three times over.

163
00:14:23,625 --> 00:14:25,250
Level one.

164
00:14:25,250 --> 00:14:35,075
Take a window of thirteen residues of the profile, feed it to a network with a hidden layer, and get three numbers out: how much this looks like helix, strand and coil.

165
00:14:35,075 --> 00:14:36,750
Level two.

166
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:43,625
Now take the outputs of level one for seventeen consecutive positions, and feed those to another network.

167
00:14:43,625 --> 00:14:45,525
What is this for?

168
00:14:45,525 --> 00:14:55,925
Level one labels each residue independently, and so it produces things that cannot exist: a single helix residue between two coils, or a helix of length two.

169
00:14:55,925 --> 00:15:02,775
Level two looks at a stretch of predictions and cleans them up, because it can learn what plausible segments look like.

170
00:15:02,775 --> 00:15:06,325
In effect it learns that structure comes in runs.

171
00:15:06,325 --> 00:15:08,050
Level three.

172
00:15:08,050 --> 00:15:12,675
A jury: several networks, trained differently, and their outputs averaged.

173
00:15:12,675 --> 00:15:21,375
This is the same idea as the ensemble that got ResNet its best ImageNet score, and it reliably buys a point or two for almost no thought.

174
00:15:21,375 --> 00:15:27,975
PHD also reports a reliability index for each residue, which says how confident it is there.

175
00:15:27,975 --> 00:15:30,975
That is a good habit and still too rare.

176
00:15:30,975 --> 00:15:41,250
And the summary of the whole thing: a better input, which is the profile, plus a flexible model, which is picture two, did what additive scores on single sequences could not.

177
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:46,025
Let us ground this in the protein we have been following all course.

178
00:15:46,025 --> 00:15:51,075
This is chain B of haemoglobin, the beta chain, structure two H H B.

179
00:15:51,075 --> 00:15:56,725
A hundred and forty-six residues, of which a hundred and seventeen are in helices.

180
00:15:56,725 --> 00:16:03,975
No strands at all, which is why it is a poor example for showing all three classes but a good one for everything else.

181
00:16:03,975 --> 00:16:13,225
On the slide, every residue carries its label, and you can see the helices as long runs of the same letter, separated by short coil stretches.

182
00:16:13,225 --> 00:16:21,150
This is what the prediction target actually looks like: not a property of the protein, but a string of labels as long as the sequence.

183
00:16:21,150 --> 00:16:31,425
The haem group, the iron-containing ring that carries oxygen, sits in a pocket between the helices, held by histidine sixty-three above it and histidine ninety-two below.

184
00:16:31,425 --> 00:16:41,250
Highlighted in orange is the thirteen-residue window centred on histidine sixty-three: the six residues before it, the histidine itself, and the six after.

185
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:46,825
That is exactly what PHD sees when it decides the label of that one residue.

186
00:16:46,825 --> 00:16:54,625
It does not see the haem, and it does not see histidine ninety-two, which is twenty-nine residues away and right next to it in space.

187
00:16:54,625 --> 00:16:59,775
That is worth noticing, because it tells you what these methods can and cannot know.

188
00:17:00,375 --> 00:17:05,850
The same two-step story, on a second biological problem, in less than two minutes.

189
00:17:05,850 --> 00:17:12,050
A signal peptide is a short stretch at the start of a protein that marks it for export out of the cell.

190
00:17:12,050 --> 00:17:14,875
You met the motif in Session four.

191
00:17:14,875 --> 00:17:17,950
Nineteen eighty-six, Gunnar von Heijne.

192
00:17:17,950 --> 00:17:23,675
A weight matrix covering positions minus thirteen to plus two around the cleavage site.

193
00:17:23,675 --> 00:17:30,175
You slide it along the first forty residues of the protein and put the cleavage site where the sum is highest.

194
00:17:30,175 --> 00:17:33,150
That is one weighted sum per window.

195
00:17:33,150 --> 00:17:34,800
Picture one.

196
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:39,175
And like every profile, it treats the positions as independent.

197
00:17:39,175 --> 00:17:42,750
Nineteen ninety-seven, SignalP version one.

198
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:45,725
Neural networks on sliding windows.

199
00:17:45,725 --> 00:17:55,775
Two of them, in fact: one that scores whether each position is inside a signal peptide, and one that recognises the cleavage site itself, with the two combined.

200
00:17:55,775 --> 00:17:57,375
Picture two.

201
00:17:57,375 --> 00:18:09,500
And because it has a hidden layer, it can learn things the weight matrix cannot express, for instance that a good cleavage site only counts as good if there is a hydrophobic stretch some distance upstream of it.

202
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:15,400
That is a dependency between positions, which is the Bulyk lesson from the start of this block.

203
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:18,750
One caution, which Session eight will make formal.

204
00:18:18,750 --> 00:18:29,625
The early networks here were trained on a few dozen sequences, and with that little data a model with a hidden layer will cheerfully memorise its training set and look far better than it is.

205
00:18:29,625 --> 00:18:35,900
That failure has a name, overfitting, and the techniques for detecting it are Session eight's subject.

206
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:42,425
And in Session eleven we return to signal peptides a third time, with a protein language model.

207
00:18:43,025 --> 00:18:47,900
Now to your lab, and to the experiment that makes this block's claim testable.

208
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:51,250
Two views of the same protein are on the slide.

209
00:18:51,250 --> 00:19:00,875
The top view is PHD's: the sequence in order, with a label for every residue, and the window the method reads to decide the label of histidine sixty-three.

210
00:19:00,875 --> 00:19:05,925
The bottom view is the one your lab uses: the amino-acid composition.

211
00:19:05,925 --> 00:19:13,500
Twenty numbers for the whole protein, saying what fraction of its residues are alanine, what fraction are cysteine, and so on.

212
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:21,300
And one target for the whole protein: the fraction of residues that are in a helix, which for this protein is nought point eight zero.

213
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:28,900
Before the next slide, the question: which of those two views keeps the information you need to say what histidine sixty-three is doing?

214
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:31,675
Composition keeps counts only.

215
00:19:31,675 --> 00:19:35,325
It knows how much of each amino acid is present.

216
00:19:35,325 --> 00:19:38,800
The window keeps order, and neighbours, and position.

217
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:43,675
Think about what that difference must mean for what each view can possibly predict.

218
00:19:44,275 --> 00:19:49,150
And here is the result from the lab, which is the punchline of the whole session.

219
00:19:49,150 --> 00:19:56,925
On the composition data, predicting helix fraction, a linear model and a multilayer perceptron score essentially the same.

220
00:19:56,925 --> 00:19:58,000
A tie.

221
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,875
Now, after everything in Block two, that should surprise you.

222
00:20:01,875 --> 00:20:05,525
The multilayer perceptron is strictly more powerful.

223
00:20:05,525 --> 00:20:10,075
It can represent everything the linear model can and a great deal more.

224
00:20:10,075 --> 00:20:12,725
How can more power buy nothing?

225
00:20:12,725 --> 00:20:17,350
The answer is in the input, and here is the cleanest way to see it.

226
00:20:17,350 --> 00:20:23,650
Take any protein and scramble its sequence: shuffle the residues into a completely random order.

227
00:20:23,650 --> 00:20:29,925
The three- dimensional structure is destroyed; the helix fraction of the scrambled thing is meaningless.

228
00:20:29,925 --> 00:20:36,875
But the composition vector is identical, because composition is just counts and counts do not care about order.

229
00:20:36,875 --> 00:20:44,750
So every model that takes composition as input must give the scrambled protein exactly the same prediction as the real one.

230
00:20:44,750 --> 00:20:46,450
Every model.

231
00:20:46,450 --> 00:20:51,700
The linear one, the multilayer perceptron, and any deep network you care to build.

232
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:59,925
The information about order has been destroyed before the model ever sees the data, and no model recovers what is not in its input.

233
00:20:59,925 --> 00:21:03,575
That is the sentence to take away from Session seven.

234
00:21:03,575 --> 00:21:18,575
And notice that it is now the fourth time this morning: the sequence logo that was flat while the pair determined binding; XOR made easy by adding one product term; PHD's jump from profiles rather than from a better network; and now your own lab.

235
00:21:18,575 --> 00:21:21,000
Same lesson, four times.

236
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:27,625
Which brings us to your capstone project, because the project is exactly this question asked of you.

237
00:21:27,625 --> 00:21:30,825
Build your own secondary-structure predictor.

238
00:21:30,825 --> 00:21:35,200
Three decisions, and the slide lists them in the order of importance.

239
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:37,575
First, what is your input?

240
00:21:37,575 --> 00:21:43,375
Single sequence, a profile from an alignment, or an embedding from a protein language model?

241
00:21:43,375 --> 00:21:48,450
After this block you should expect that choice to matter more than anything else you decide.

242
00:21:48,450 --> 00:21:50,450
Second, which model?

243
00:21:50,450 --> 00:21:56,700
Logistic regression, a network with a hidden layer, something convolutional after Session ten.

244
00:21:56,700 --> 00:22:02,825
And third, the question most students get wrong, and the one I care about most: how do you test it?

245
00:22:02,825 --> 00:22:12,650
Specifically, is your test set made of proteins the model has never seen, in the sense that matters, which is that they are not homologs of anything in your training set?

246
00:22:12,650 --> 00:22:19,475
If a close relative of a test protein sat in your training data, your reported accuracy is measuring memory.

247
00:22:19,475 --> 00:22:26,725
That is Session eight's subject, and it is the single most common way that published results in our field turn out to be wrong.

248
00:22:26,725 --> 00:22:35,100
For calibration: Chou-Fasman and GOR, which we met earlier, are around fifty to sixty-five per cent and are good baselines to beat.

249
00:22:35,100 --> 00:22:43,475
Modern methods that combine profiles or language models with deep networks reach roughly eighty-five per cent on standard benchmarks.

250
00:22:43,475 --> 00:22:47,325
You will not reach eighty-five, and you are not expected to.

251
00:22:47,325 --> 00:22:54,300
You are expected to make a defensible choice of input, model and test, and to report honestly what you got.

252
00:22:54,900 --> 00:23:01,200
One last piece of evidence, from the present day, because this is today's lesson in its strongest form.

253
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:14,475
ProtTrans is a family of protein language models: networks trained on hundreds of millions of protein sequences, with no labels, to produce a vector of numbers for each residue that captures something about its context.

254
00:23:14,475 --> 00:23:17,625
We build these properly in Session eleven.

255
00:23:17,625 --> 00:23:23,375
For now, treat such a model as a very sophisticated replacement for one-hot encoding.

256
00:23:23,375 --> 00:23:31,700
Now take plain logistic regression, which is one weighted sum and a sigmoid, picture one, the simplest model in this entire course.

257
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:36,225
Put it on top of those embeddings, with no alignment of any kind.

258
00:23:36,225 --> 00:23:44,075
It reaches between seventy-four point three and seventy-nine point three per cent three-state accuracy across several test sets.

259
00:23:44,075 --> 00:23:51,750
Compare that to PHD's seventy point eight per cent, which took a multiple alignment, three levels of networks and a jury.

260
00:23:51,750 --> 00:24:02,925
The comparison is indicative rather than exact, because PHD's number is on its own nineteen ninety-three test set and these are on modern ones, so do not quote it as a head-to-head.

261
00:24:02,925 --> 00:24:10,675
But the size of the thing is clear: picture one, on a good enough input, is competitive with a careful nineteen-nineties pipeline.

262
00:24:10,675 --> 00:24:12,700
Go a little further.

263
00:24:12,700 --> 00:24:24,675
Put a small convolutional network, Session ten's subject, on top of better embeddings, and you reach eighty-four point three per cent, which equals a method that uses full multiple sequence alignments.

264
00:24:24,675 --> 00:24:29,175
So the question the slide asks is a real one, not a rhetorical one.

265
00:24:29,175 --> 00:24:33,425
If the input is good enough, how much model do you actually need?

266
00:24:33,425 --> 00:24:39,575
And the honest answer is: less than you think, but the good input took an enormous model to produce.

267
00:24:39,575 --> 00:24:41,975
The work did not disappear.

268
00:24:41,975 --> 00:24:43,525
It moved.

269
00:24:44,125 --> 00:24:48,075
Back to the slide from the beginning, and the question I promised.

270
00:24:48,075 --> 00:24:52,750
What changed between the two pictures, and why does that allow XOR?

271
00:24:52,750 --> 00:24:58,350
Let me answer it, and if you can already answer it yourself, this session has done its job.

272
00:24:58,350 --> 00:25:01,425
Picture one is a single weighted sum.

273
00:25:01,425 --> 00:25:07,950
Whatever its weights, it draws one straight boundary, and it decides by which side of that boundary you are on.

274
00:25:07,950 --> 00:25:13,150
That is enough for AND, for OR, for NAND, and for a great many real problems.

275
00:25:13,150 --> 00:25:22,350
It is not enough for XOR, because XOR's two positive cases sit on opposite corners and no straight line separates them from the other two.

276
00:25:22,350 --> 00:25:29,575
Picture two has a layer of units in the middle, each drawing its own straight boundary, and an output unit that combines them.

277
00:25:29,575 --> 00:25:34,825
Two boundaries can carve out a band, which is not something one boundary can do.

278
00:25:34,825 --> 00:25:37,675
That is why it can do XOR.

279
00:25:37,675 --> 00:25:45,250
And the essential detail, the one people forget: it only works because there is a non-linear activation between the layers.

280
00:25:45,250 --> 00:25:51,775
Without it, the layers collapse into a single weighted sum and you are back in picture one with extra steps.

281
00:25:51,775 --> 00:25:57,225
Then the third thing, which is not on either picture and is the reason Block three exists.

282
00:25:57,225 --> 00:26:00,800
Both pictures can only use what is in their input.

283
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:04,875
A hidden layer finds combinations of the features you gave it.

284
00:26:04,875 --> 00:26:09,525
It cannot find combinations of features you destroyed before it saw them.

285
00:26:09,525 --> 00:26:12,200
Model, activation, input.

286
00:26:12,200 --> 00:26:13,725
Those three.

287
00:26:14,325 --> 00:26:16,550
That is Session seven.

288
00:26:16,550 --> 00:26:23,475
Session eight takes the thing you built today and asks the question we have been carefully avoiding: how do you know it works?

289
00:26:23,475 --> 00:26:36,600
Training, validation and test sets, what the different evaluation numbers actually measure, and the trap that catches almost everyone in our field, which is a test set full of proteins that are close relatives of the training set.

290
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:43,350
It also hands you PyTorch properly, so that you stop writing training loops by hand and start writing models.

291
00:26:43,350 --> 00:26:48,225
And it begins with blood cells, which will be our example for three sessions.

292
00:26:48,225 --> 00:26:57,150
The Session seven page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

293
00:26:57,150 --> 00:27:01,200
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

294
00:27:01,200 --> 00:27:02,825
See you there.

